WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.640
A local model file contains model weights, usually stored as tensors.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.810
Tensors are structured arrays of numbers that represent what the model learned during

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:09.440
training or fine-tuning.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.334
Those numbers are not useful in isolation.

00:00:11.334 --> 00:00:15.710
The runtime has to know the model architecture, tensor names, tensor shapes, precision,

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:19.550
and layout so it can map the file contents into the correct computation.

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:22.560
Metadata turns a model file into something a runtime can reason about.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:26.750
It can describe architecture information, context length, tokenizer details,

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:31.355
quantization type, special tokens, model family, and sometimes prompt formatting hints.

00:00:31.355 --> 00:00:34.240
This is why a model file is more than a single blob of numbers.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:38.060
It is a package of data and instructions that helps the runtime load and use the model

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:39.335
consistently.

00:00:39.335 --> 00:00:43.960
Tokenizer data is especially important because models do not process text as raw words.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.860
Text is broken into tokens, and generated tokens are converted back into text.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:49.450
The vocabulary, special tokens,

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:52.280
and tokenizer behavior need to match what the model expects.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:53.470
If the tokenizer is wrong,

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:57.470
prompt text may be represented differently than the model was trained to understand,

00:00:57.470 --> 00:00:59.660
which can lead to weak or unexpected output.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:02.865
Runtime compatibility depends on both the file and the inference engine.

00:01:02.865 --> 00:01:07.570
A GGUF file may be valid, but an older runtime might not support a newer architecture,

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.860
metadata field, or quantization type.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:10.220
Likewise,

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:14.640
a runtime may support the architecture but require specific settings to perform well.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.455
Local inference is a combination of model file, runtime capability, hardware fit,

00:01:18.455 --> 00:01:19.520
and configuration.
